[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: One grammar, not one per plan and one per specification

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-ONE-GRAMMAR
TITLE: One grammar, not one per plan and one per specification
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: A consumer needs a vocabulary this repository's grammar cannot express without weakening it for everyone else.
STATEMENT: >>>
There is ONE grammar. A plan and a specification share a vocabulary; they are
not two languages.

A sketch on this branch split them in two -- a `plan` grammar of PLAN and WORK
beside a `spec` grammar of the content types -- on the reasoning that plans are
disposable and specifications are durable, so a finished plan could be collected
without losing what it produced. That reasoning does not survive contact with
two facts already settled here.

FIRST, building a plan PRODUCES content nodes. "Implement this mechanism", "we
decided this", "here is the evidence for that design" -- the durable types
appear inside the planning work, not only after it. DEC-PLAN-LIFECYCLE-OPEN
already narrowed to exactly this: plans are not structurally different from
specifications, and the only open question is retention.

SECOND, with one node per file and containment carried by an edge, there are no
documents to belong to. Which plan a node belongs to is already a relation. So
the split was never about where a node lives; it was about which vocabulary
exists, and there is only one.

The payoff is that collection gets SIMPLER rather than harder. Dropping a
finished plan removes the plan node and its work nodes; the content nodes lose
one edge and remain. DISSOLUTION IS EDGE REMOVAL, NOT FILE MOVEMENT -- nothing
migrates, no identity has to survive a move, and there is no moment where a node
exists in two places.

Two grammars would also have made a node's grammar its home, reintroducing the
location concept that one-node-per-file removed.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Neither shape has been used yet, so the tie is broken on the standing
preference: simpler until a forcing function appears. None does. Variable
planning granularity, the one candidate, is a question of how many work nodes
get written and not of which vocabulary exists.

The distinction the split was reaching for is real, but it is between the
IMPERATIVE and a CLAIM, which DEC-NODE-TYPE-GRID already draws with two node
types instead of two grammars.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
The two-grammar sketch is committed at packages/strictdoc-grammar/values/plan.nix and values/spec.nix, wired to nothing, with its devenv block commented out. It stays as a written-out alternative rather than being deleted, and MECH-LAYER-ZERO-GRAMMAR-ADOPTION carries what it held open. PLAN and WORK still have to be added to the one grammar, and whether both survive beads is DEC-PLAN-LIFECYCLE-OPEN's.
<<<
